WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.724
I am on a wireless router and I'm trying to decide which type of network security, specifically which form of encryption, to use. Let's go through some of these choices.

00:09.724 --> 00:20.317
WEP is Wired Equivalent Privacy. This form of encryption is considered weak as it has actually been cracked. Don't use this if you don't have to.

00:20.317 --> 00:29.175
WPA is Wi-Fi Protected Access. This is much stronger than WEP and may be the strongest form of encryption you have available,

00:29.175 --> 00:33.522
especially if the wireless access point you are configuring is old.

00:33.522 --> 00:43.819
WPA2 is currently the strongest form of encryption and this should be used whenever possible. It is available on this wireless access point, so I will use it here.

00:43.819 --> 00:55.871
Another option for securing wireless networks is to use the 802.11x option, also known as port-based network access control. This can be used for wireless or wired LANs.

00:55.871 --> 01:06.649
802.11x has three pieces: the supplicant, the authenticator, and the network. Here is how this works. A remote client accesses a network remotely.

01:06.649 --> 01:14.089
This client is known as the supplicant. Next, a router, known as an authenticator, alerts an authentication server,

01:14.089 --> 01:20.189
such as a RADIUS server, to let it know a client wants to get on the network. The client will enter some credentials.

01:20.189 --> 01:28.578
The server will verify those credentials and, if they are correct, will allow the client onto the network.

